;;; A string written in a guard never equals a value — **W18**, register
;;; regression `str-in-guard` (`corpus/regressions.toml`), the case's
;;; mechanism in a guard.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, one over the atom `a` and one over the string `"b"`.
;;; `g` keeps the `holds` value that equals `"b"`, and `n` every one that
;;; does not.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), written
;;; only after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`: the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at its first string, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one, `g` never fired and
;;; `n` fired on both: the rule compiler lowered a guard's string to a raw
;;; node, which equals no value (`compile.rs`' `guard_arg`, `match_.rs`'
;;; `resolved_eq`), so `eq` never held and `neq` always did. Root derived
;;; `(other a)` and `(other b)` and no `hit`; the candidates were `(hit a)`
;;; and `(hit b)`, and `ein solve FILE --minimal` printed root's state as
;;; `(model)`, over `hit` alone. Today the program is refused at the parse:
;;; *unexpected `"b"`: a string*.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node) (relation other Node)
(holds a) (holds "b")
(rule g () :match (and (holds ?x) (eq ?x "b")) :assert (hit ?x))
(rule n () :match (and (holds ?x) (neq ?x "b")) :assert (other ?x))
